-- title:
--   A uniform 2-adic exponent for torsion annihilators on J₀(p)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime. Write $\mathbb{T}_{\mathrm{abs}} =$ `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb{Z}[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ for the abstract polynomial Hecke algebra, and let `JZero p` be the group of degree-zero divisor classes of the modular function field of level $p$ base changed to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, i.e. the points of the Jacobian of $X_0(p)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; it carries the `HeckeAlg`-module structure `heckeModuleBar p`, given, since the geometric Hecke operators commute ([`ModularCurve.heckeOperatorsCommuteBar`](thm.html#ModularCurve.heckeOperatorsCommuteBar)), by evaluating $X_\ell$ at the Hecke operator indexed by $\ell$. On the other side, `heckeEvalForms p 2` is the ring homomorphism from $\mathbb{T}_{\mathrm{abs}}$ to the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra `heckeAlgebra p 2 ∅` of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(S_2(\Gamma_0(p)))$ generated by the Hecke generators, sending $X_\ell$ to $U_\ell$ when $\ell \mid p$ and to $T_\ell$ otherwise, and `latticeRestrictHom p ∅` restricts such an operator to the integral lattice `intLattice p 2` (the $\mathbb{Z}$-span of the cusp forms all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers), landing in the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{T} =$ `heckeLatticeAlgebra p ∅` of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{Z}}(\mathrm{intLattice}\,p\,2)$ cut out as the range of that restriction. The assertion is that there exists a natural number $d$ such that for all natural numbers $m$ and all $u \in \mathbb{T}_{\mathrm{abs}}$: if $u$ annihilates every point $y$ of the Jacobian with $2^{m+d} \cdot y = 0$, then the image of $u$ in $\mathbb{T}$ under the composite of `heckeEvalForms p 2` with `latticeRestrictHom p ∅` lies in the ideal of $\mathbb{T}$ generated by $2^m$. The exponent $d$ is uniform in $m$ and in $u$.
--
--   This is a divisibility (saturation) statement comparing the annihilator of the $2$-power torsion of $J_0(p)$ with $2$-power divisibility inside the Hecke algebra acting on the integral lattice of weight-two cusp forms of level $p$, in the spirit of Mazur's identification of the Hecke algebra with an endomorphism ring of $J_0(p)$, but with the exponent $d$ left unspecified. It is used in the construction of suitable $2^n$-multiples of idempotent-like generators in the tower of Hecke algebras acting on Tate modules, namely by [`ModularCurve.exists_nsmul_generator_idempotent_tower_heckeAlg_tateModule_inf_pi_closure_inertia_smul_sub`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_nsmul_generator_idempotent_tower_heckeAlg_tateModule_inf_pi_closure_inertia_smul_sub).
